{{Short description|Marxist political faction in Germany}}
The '''Lensch-Cunow-Haenisch group''' was a political faction within the [[Social Democratic Party of Germany]] founded in 1915 by [[Revisionism_(Marxism)#19th_century|anti-revisionist]] Marxists who despite previously opposing participation in the [[First World War]] now supported it. Its name comes from the three principal protagonists: [[Paul Lensch]], [[Heinrich Cunow]] and [[Konrad Haenisch]]. They followed the initiative of [[Parvus]] in advocating a German victory as a positive step for [[social democracy|international social democracy]]. They mobilised Marxist arguments behind the war effort.<ref name="Macnair">{{cite book |last1=Macnair |first1=Mike |title=Kautsky on Colonialism |date=21 February 2013 |publisher=Lulu.com |isbn=9781291329810 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=OUCrBAAAQBAJ&dq=Lensch-Cunow-Haenisch+group&pg=PA33 |accessdate=28 June 2018 |language=en}}</ref>

In 1907 Parvus had been a critic of [[German imperialism]], publishing ''Colonial Policy and the Breakdown'' during the [[1907 German federal election]], often referred to as the Hottentot Election.<ref name="Lewis">{{cite news |last1=Lewis |first1=Benjamin |title=World War I: The SPD left's dirty secret |url=https://thecharnelhouse.org/2014/06/29/world-war-i-the-spd-lefts-dirty-secret/#more-21124 |access-date=21 February 2021 |work=Weekly Worker |issue=1016, 26 June, 2014 |publisher=1016 June 26, 2014 |date=2014}}</ref>

