{{Short description|Type of periodical from World War I}}
[[File:The Wipers Times 1916-03-20 p1.jpg|thumb|Using humor to deal with difficult circumstances: the English trench newspaper ''[[The Wipers Times]]'' is offering [[flamethrowers]] up for sale as toys.]]
[[File:Reading the news in the trenches 1000 yards from the Bosch present positions (4688659754).jpg|thumb|Soldiers reading a newspaper in a trench during the [[Battle of Messines (1917)]], {{convert|1000|yd|m}} from the enemy]]
A '''trench newspaper''' or '''front newspaper''' is a type of periodical that came into being during the [[World War I|First World War]]. Trench newspapers were produced for soldiers stationed at the [[Western Front (World War I)|Western Front]], which had become bogged down in a [[Trench Warfare|trench war]]. They differ from the official military newspapers in that they were produced within the ranks or by private citizens.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Jacques |first1=Boyer |title=Newspapers That Are Printed Within Gun Range of the Enemy |journal=Scientific American |date=June 24, 1916 |volume=114 |issue=26 |pages=665–672 |doi=10.1038/scientificamerican06241916-665 |jstor=26014730 |bibcode=1916SciAm.114..665B |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/26014730?seq=1 |access-date=March 28, 2024}}</ref>

==Content==
Main topic of the trench newspapers were the soldiers themselves and the conditions they found themselves in. News from the soldiers' home regions also featured prominently, especially for Belgian soldiers, who were completely cut off from their mostly occupied country. Trench newspapers offered some relief to the soldiers and helped to keep morale high. From the trench newspapers, a lot can be learned about life at the front.

==Production==
The production and distribution of trench newspapers was dependent on the situation at the front and the available supplies. The French troops were using gum to be able to copy newspapers. Many were published for a short while only, and those that lasted appeared intermittently. Still, some continued to be published after the war was over. Where no [[printing press]] was available, trench papers were typed or handwritten, and then multiplied using a [[Mimeograph|mimeograph machine]].

